/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Carry select splits an n-bit adder into blocks of k bits.
   - For each sum bit in the block, a multiplexer picks the output of the correct adder.
   - For a = 1011 and b = 0110 in two-bit blocks, the low block gives sum 01 with carry 1.
   - Area is the cost.
   - Carry skip, also called carry bypass, uses the propagate signal pi = ai XOR bi from Chapter 4.1.
   - If P = 1, every bit in the block propagates.
   - The block size k sets the delay.
   - Tskip ≈ 2k·tc + (n/k − 1)·tmux, kopt = √(n·tmux ÷ 2tc)
   - Here n is the adder width in bits; k is the block width in bits; 2k·tc is the ripple in the first and last blocks; n/k − 1 is the number of bypass multiplexers on the path; kopt is where the two terms balance (set dT/dk = 0).
   - Small blocks skip often but put many multiplexers in the path; large blocks ripple too far.
*/
